  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe core idea is to employ a hybrid evolutionary-local optimization framework that combines global exploration via differential evolution (DE) with precise refinement through L-BFGS-B. This addresses the limitations of prior methods by leveraging DE's robustness to avoid local optima, followed by local optimization to exploit promising candidates. The method uses a novel initialization based on binary sequences with adaptive perturbations to balance exploration and constraint satisfaction. The key innovation is the explicit handling of the integral constraint through normalization during DE iterations and the use of a tailored objective function that directly evaluates the maximum overlap.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nWe represent h as a piecewise constant function with n_points samples over [0, 2], satisfying:\n- 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 for all i\n- \u2211h[i] = n_points / 2\n\nThe objective is to minimize:\nC\u2085 = max_k \u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x)(1 - h(x + k)) dx\nThis is equivalent to:\nC\u2085 = max_lag (np.correlate(h, 1 - h, mode=\"full\") * dx)\n\nConstraints:\n- h \u2208 [0, 1]\u207f_points\n- \u2211h = n_points / 2\n\nThe algorithm uses a two-phase optimization:\n- Phase 1: Parallelized global search via DE, enforcing the \u2211h constraint through normalization\n- Phase 2: Local refinement via L-BFGS-B, using the exact correlation metric\n\nThe DE objective function computes the unnormalized max_lag value, while the L-BFGS-B objective is the negative of this metric to convert minimization to maximization.\n\n3. Implementation procedure\nStage 1: Parent validation and initialization\n- If initial_h_values is provided, verify \u2211h \u2248 n_points/2 within 1e-6 tolerance\n- If no parent, generate a binary sequence with:\n   1. Randomly set half the entries to 1 and the rest to 0\n   2. Apply a small sinusoidal perturbation to avoid symmetry\n   3. Normalize to \u2211h = n_points / 2\n\nStage 2: Differential evolution global search\n- Initialize a population of 30 candidate vectors\n- For each candidate:\n   1. Clip values to [0, 1]\n   2. Normalize to \u2211h = n_points / 2\n   3. Compute the unnormalized max_lag value\n- Run DE with:\n   - Population size: 30\n   - Mutation factor: 0.5\n   - Crossover probability: 0.9\n   - Max iterations: 100\n   - Termination condition: 100 iterations or 50% improvement in max_lag\n\nStage 3: Local refinement with L-BFGS-B\n- Use best DE candidate as initial guess\n- Apply L-BFGS-B with:\n   1. Objective: -max_lag (to minimize negative value)\n   2. Bounds: [0, 1] for all elements\n   3. Constraint: \u2211h = n_points / 2 (equality constraint)\n   4. Max iterations: 500\n   5. Tolerance: 1e-8\n- During optimization, apply:\n   a. Adaptive step size scaling based on gradient magnitude\n   b. Periodic random perturbations to avoid premature convergence\n\nStage 4: Final evaluation and fallback\n- Re-evaluate the best solution using the exact correlation function\n- Cross-check with a fresh L-BFGS-B run\n- If time remains, perform 50 additional L-BFGS-B iterations\n- If solution fails constraints, fall back to parent vector scaled to current n_points\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\nDefault configuration:\n- n_points = 600 (provides better resolution than 400)\n- DE population size: 30\n- Mutation factor: 0.5\n- Crossover probability: 0.9\n- Initial random seed: 42\n- L-BFGS-B max iterations: 500\n- Perturbation frequency: 5% every 100 iterations\n\nVariations:\n- If parent exists: apply 5% sinusoidal perturbation\n- For n_points < 200: interleave with a higher frequency pattern\n- If convergence stalls: increase DE population size to 40\n- Use adaptive mutation factor (0.5 to 0.8 based on progress)\n- For failed optimizations, fallback to parent vector scaled to current n_points\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\nTime allocation:\n- 250s for DE global search\n- 300s for L-BFGS-B refinement\n- 50s for final evaluation and fallback\n\nRobustness measures:\n- Clamp all values to [0, 1] after each update\n- If \u2211h deviates by >1e-5, apply a rescaling factor\n- Track top 5 candidates and re-evaluate them if optimization stalls\n- Use early stopping if C\u2085 improves by <1e-5 over 10 iterations\n- For invalid proposals, apply a 0.1% damping factor\n\n6. Validation and return contract\nFeasibility checks:\n- Verify all h[i] \u2208 [0, 1] within 1e-6\n- Confirm \u2211h == n_points / 2 within 1e-6\n- Ensure C\u2085 is a finite positive number\n\nFinal validation:\n- Re-evaluate the candidate using the exact correlation function\n- Cross-check with a fresh minimize call\n- Ensure the return shape is exactly (n_points,) for h_values\n\nSanity checks:\n- Confirm no NaNs or infinities in the result\n- Validate that the correlation calculation matches the evaluator\n- Ensure the return shape is exactly (n_points,) for h_values",
